\section*{Limitations}
\label{sec:limitations}

\paragraph{Full-replacement vs.\ accumulation.}
The primary benchmark uses full-replacement chains; real-world contamination involves accumulation at unknown mixing ratios \cite{gerstgrasser2024_accumulate}.
At 25--50\% synthetic mixing, accuracy drops 8--15\,pp and $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}{=}3$ is preserved in 5/6 tested cells (GPT-2~XL nucleus at 50\% drops to $\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}{=}2$; d2v3 AUC\,=\,0.695, marginally below threshold; Table~\ref{tab:accum_auc}); extension to larger models and higher ratios remains future work.

\paragraph{Scale and generalization boundaries.}
At 14B, cross-reference accuracy drops to 44.1\% ($\mathrm{EDD}_{0.70}{=}2$), with reference-model mismatch accounting for ${\sim}$7\,pp; cross-strategy transfer (d1--d3: 45.9\%) can invert d1/d2 predictions (d1v2 AUC as low as 0.13 for temperature text classified by a nucleus-trained model), requiring strategy-aware deployment.
Web-crawl false positive rates (15--34\% base rate across C4, FineWeb, and RedPajama) are partially resolved by confidence thresholding ($T{=}0.10$ reduces FPR to 0.1--7\%) but not fully eliminated.

\paragraph{Reference model dependency.}
The pipeline requires a fixed reference model; cross-architecture references degrade accuracy by 4--11\,pp (Appendix~\ref{app:ref_ablation}), and no principled selection criterion exists beyond empirical ablation.
Additional caveats (single LoRA seed with ${\sim}$1--3\,pp uncertainty, adversarial robustness, passage length sensitivity) are detailed in the Appendix.

\section*{Ethics Statement}

This work uses publicly available pretrained language models (Pythia-1.4B, OLMo-1B, GPT-2~XL) and Wikipedia text as seed data.
No human subjects were involved, no private or sensitive data were collected, and no new models were released.
Our method is intended to support data provenance and training-data auditing; we acknowledge that depth estimation tools could in principle be misused to circumvent content moderation, though such risks are shared with all detection-oriented research.
Code and data will be released upon acceptance.
AI assistants were used in an auxiliary role for coding support and language polishing.
All scientific decisions, experimental design, analysis, and core writing were conducted by the authors.

\section{Generalization and Robustness}
\label{sec:generalization}

We test whether atlas accuracy (68.9--85.9\% four-class; d1--d3: 58.9--81.5\%) reflects genuine statistical regularities across eight generalization axes (five below, three in Appendix).

\subsection{Classifier Comparison and Surface-Text Ablation}
\label{sec:gen:baselines}

Table~\ref{tab:baselines} compares methods across all 9 cells.
Four classifiers (RF, XGBoost, ordinal MLP, prototypical) converge within 0.8\,pp under cross-model transfer (Appendix~\ref{app:learned_estimator}), confirming a feature-determined accuracy ceiling.

\paragraph{Neural end-to-end baselines.}
DeBERTa-v3-base (LoRA) underperforms RF by 16--32\,pp (d2 recall 8.4\%); RoBERTa-DCOL (ordinal contrastive; 64.5\%) narrows the gap but remains 14\,pp below RF.

\paragraph{Binary detectors and membership inference.}
Perturbation-based (DetectGPT, 28.8\%) and membership-inference (Min-K\% Prob, 33.2\%; Min-K\%++ \cite{zhang2024_minkpp} comparable) methods produce scores with no ordinal resolution (Table~\ref{tab:baselines}); pairwise analysis confirms ordinal collapse: Binoculars d1v2 AUC\,=\,0.50--0.53 vs.\ RF 0.93 (Appendix~\ref{app:detector_baselines}).
CORAL calibration does not close the gap (d1--d3: 40.4\% vs.\ RF 71.6\%).
Binoculars \cite{binoculars2024}, calibrated with 11-feature RF, independently converges to 78.1\% ($p{>}0.05$ vs.\ our 78.4\%); merging all 30 features yields 82.6\% (Appendix~\ref{app:feature_ablation_bino}).

\paragraph{Surface-text ablation.}
Single-feature regression on mean perplexity performs at chance (25.1\%); zero-shot LLMs (best 36.2\%; Appendix~\ref{app:zeroshot_prompt}) also fail; depth signal resides in distributional statistics inaccessible from surface text.

